  "prompt": "prompts/physics_committee_terra_round2_v1.md",
  "preferred_configuration": {
    "prize_parts": [
      {
        "ballot_id": "B014",
        "laureates": [
          "Bertrand I. Halperin",
          "David R. Nelson",
          "A. Peter Young"
        ],
        "citation": "for the defect-mediated theory of two-dimensional melting and prediction of the hexatic phase"
      }
    ]
  },
  "alternatives": [
    "B022",
    "B028"
  ],
  "statement": {
    "case_for": "My configuration is unchanged. B014 identifies a concise, physically intelligible mechanism: topological-defect unbinding accounts for two-dimensional melting and entails the distinctive prediction of an intervening hexatic phase. This is not simply a useful calculational framework. It gave a durable organizing principle for simulations, colloids, films, and other two-dimensional systems, while retaining a precise domain of validity. Halperin, Nelson, and Young provide a coherent three-person account of the theory and its essential predictions.",
    "case_against": "The two-step scenario is not realized identically in every material; disorder, interaction details, and nonideal conditions may instead yield first-order behavior. The citation must consequently reward the defect-mediated mechanism and hexatic prediction, not claim a universal melting route. The required historical review should also confirm the exact respective contributions of Halperin, Nelson, and Young.",
    "responses": [
      {
        "member_id": "kroll-stefan",
        "point": "Kroll argues that B022 has independently reproducible 10^-18 performance, compact Katori-Ye attribution, and demonstrated use as a probe of gravity and fundamental physics.",
        "response": "I accept that B022 is the strongest experimental alternative and have retained it as such. Its reproducibility answers an important maturity question. But the proposed recognition still needs to separate the lattice-clock principle and defining realization from a long sequence of later precision advances, and its consequences beyond metrology remain less conceptually settled than B014's longstanding mechanism-to-prediction-to-observation chain."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "eriksson-olle",
        "point": "Eriksson regards B028 as a major, mature theory for correlated electrons but cautions that it is an enabling method with a broad theoretical lineage.",
        "response": "I continue to share his high assessment of dynamical mean-field theory. Its success in connecting correlated-electron models to measurable spectra is exceptional. Yet the caveat remains important: B028 is a versatile framework whose discovery-level boundary and allocation require unusually careful formulation. B014 instead names a particular mechanism and a falsifiable phase prediction, which makes both its scientific claim and attribution more delimited."
      },
      {
        "member_id": "danielsson-ulf",
        "point": "Danielsson finds optical lattice clocks mature but emphasizes that their larger practical and fundamental consequences are still unfolding.",
        "response": "That distinction clarifies my ordering. Demonstrated precision is already prizeworthy science, but anticipated redefinition and broader applications should not bear argumentative weight before their scope is established. B014 does not depend on such prospective consequences: its explanatory and predictive role has already been tested across decades, subject to the explicitly limited claim that not every two-dimensional system follows the complete route."
      }
    ],
    "uncertainties": [
      "The archival record should establish precisely how the essential theoretical contributions are divided among Halperin, Nelson, and Young.",
      "The range of experimental systems displaying the complete two-step melting sequence remains heterogeneous and must be reflected in narrow citation wording.",
      "For B022, a final comparison requires a historical account separating Katori's principle and Ye's defining development from later precision-engineering contributions."
    ],
    "conflict_note": "No connection to any proposed laureate is apparent from the supplied profile. I continue to note institutional proximity to committee colleagues Göran Johansson and Eva Olsson through the Gothenburg physics environment, with no specific collaboration identified."
  }
}
